<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stab-in-the-back myth</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dolchstoßlegende</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The stab-in-the-back myth (German Dolchstoßlegende) was an antisemitic and anti-communist conspiracy theory, widely believed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after 1918, which held that the Imperial German Army had not lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> on the battlefield but had been betrayed on the home front, above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, revolutionary socialists and republican politicians. Its advocates called the leaders who signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a> the November criminals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> made it part of their official history after 1933. Historians reject the myth, pointing to the lack of further reserves and the overwhelming of German forces on the western front by more numerous Allied armies.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>By the later part of the war the Supreme High Command under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> controlled a large part of the economy. On the Western Front the Hindenburg Line had been penetrated, though the Allied armies had crossed the 1914 frontier in only a few places. The failure of the Spring Offensive of 1918 was blamed on strikes in the arms industry, seen as the work of treacherous elements, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> taking most of the blame.</p><p>Richard Steigmann-Gall traces the idea to a sermon of 3 February 1918 by the court chaplain Bruno Doehring, though Boris Barth says Doehring spoke only of betrayal. Barth traces the first documented use of the term to a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 2 November 1918, where Ernst Müller-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> urged listeners not to give the front a dagger-stab. In 1919 Colonel Max Bauer wrote that the war was lost only and exclusively through the failure of the homeland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The myth held that the army was undefeated in the field and was betrayed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, revolutionary socialists and the republican politicians who overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>. In October 1918 Douglas Haig said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was not broken in the military sense, yet in private he wrote that he thought the German army was beaten. Historians accept that the collapse of morale and economy at home was a factor but reject the myth. Basil Liddell Hart stressed the threat of a new Allied advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In October 1916 the army ordered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the troops to show that they were under-represented at the front; it showed the opposite, and the army suppressed the results. Charges of a Jewish conspiracy drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a>, who had a large hand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> revolution until his assassination in February 1919, and on the Bavarian Soviet Republic of 6 April to 3 May 1919, many of whose leaders were Jewish. Matthias Erzberger and Walther Rathenau were assassinated.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> made the myth part of its history of the 1920s. The Allied policy of unconditional surrender, devised in 1943, was meant in part to prevent a repetition. Richard McMasters Hunt argued that behind the belief lay a communal shame at losing the war, which became a solvent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> democracy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stab-in-the-back myth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
